## 1. Introduction
Collagen is the most abundant protein in the human body and is a major component of the extracellular matrix (ECM). Collagen is composed of three polypeptide chains that are rich in hydroxyproline amino acid [1] and are twisted into a triple-helix structure [2]. Over 20 different types of collagen have been identified in humans; types I, II and III are considered the main types, and together, they make up to $80\%$ of the body’s collagen. Types I and III are essential in wound healing [3]; however, type II is important as a predominant component of hyaline cartilage [4]. The biological characteristics of collagen such as its biodegradability and weak antigenicity give it excellent biocompatibility and safety, making collagen a primary resource for medical applications [5]. It can be used for tissue engineering, such as skin and bone substitution, artificial blood vessels and heart valves [5]. Moreover, it is believed that collagen constructs might be able to replace faulty heart valves in the future [6]. For oral administration, type II collagen has been found to reduce the symptoms of rheumatoid arthritis [7].
Currently, most commercial collagens are extracted from bovine and pig skins or chicken wastes. However, these animal sources are unsuitable for many religious and ethical reasons. In addition, pig skin has the risk of containing biological contaminants and poisons caused by the outbreak of bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE) and foot-and-mouth disease (FMD) [8,9]. Recently, collagen extracted from fish has gained more attention because fish byproducts have more collagen and are considered a safer collagen source compared to other animals [10].
Fish scales are bio-composites of highly ordered type I collagen fibres and hydroxyapatite $\mathrm{Ca}_{10}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{PO}_{4})_{6}$ [13]. Despite the potential of collecting collagen from aquatic animals, there have only been a few efforts made to obtain alternative and safe collagen from aquatic animals because of their low denaturation temperature $\left(T_{\mathrm{d}}\right)$ . $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ is the temperature at which the triple-helix structure of collagen deforms to a random coil structure [14]. Most collagen from aquatic animals has a $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ below $30^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in acidic solutions [15]. A low $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ is an undesirable property in the manufacturing process and for biomaterials because denaturation drastically alters the physicochemical, biological and mechanical properties of collagen [16]. The denaturation temperature of collagen from deep-sea redfish scales was reported to be $17.7^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ [17] and approximately $28^{\circ}C$ for carp scales [18]. In general, the $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ of collagen from marine fish scales are between 26 and $29^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ ; this makes them less thermally stable than mammalian collagen, which has a $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ approximately $41^{\circ}\mathsf C$ [19]. The thermal stability of collagen can be improved by chemical crosslinking using glutaraldehyde [20], carbodiimide [21] or physical treatments such as ultraviolet (UV) irradiation or dehydrothermal (DHT) treatment [22]. Yunoki et al. [21] improved the thermal stability of the gel produced from Salmon collagen from 28 to be $47^{\circ}\mathsf C$ by introducing a 1-ethyl3-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)carbodiimide (EDC) crosslinking agent during the fibril formation.
Although the promising results from extensive work on collagen produced from seawater fish scales were introduced to literature, more research is still needed, especially for collagen extraction from scales of freshwater fish living in warm areas, which are known to have higher thermostability due to their living environment.
Therefore, the aim of this study was to extract, purify, characterize and perform preliminary biological evaluation of type I collagen from scales of Egyptian Nile Tilapia as a novel source of fish scale collagen.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Fish scales preparation and demineralization
Fish Scales of Nile Tilapia, caught from the Nile River Delta, were randomly collected from the local market during the month of June. The scales were washed thoroughly with distilled water and stored at $-20^{\circ}C$ until used. All reagents used were of analytical grade. The fish scales were washed twice in $10\mathrm{wt}.\%$ of $_{\mathsf{N a C l}}$ solutions (scales:solution $=1{:}10$ ) to remove non-collagenous proteins on the surface by stirring the solution for $24\mathrm{h}$ , then washed thoroughly with distilled water until the pH was neutral. Demineralization was performed by stirring the scales for 90 min in $0.4\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{l}$ HCl (scales:solution $=1{:}15$ ), then washing the scales three times in distilled water to ready them for collagen extraction.
## 2.2. Extraction of pepsin-soluble collagen (PSC)
