The extraction processes were carried out at $4^{\circ}\mathsf C.$ . The demineralized scales were stirred in $0.5\mathrm{M}$ acetic acid containing pepsin (pepsin 1:3000 from HOG stomach, LobaChemie, India) for two days; the pepsin concentration was set to be $1\mathrm{wt.\%}$ of the demineralized scales. The residual scales and micro-sized residues were filtered out using a piece of gauze were then centrifuged at $14,000\times g$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ . The collagen concentration in the supernatant was roughly estimated from the dry weight. Pepsin (1 wt.% of the estimated collagen) was added to the supernatant and stirred for another two days. The soluble collagen was purified by a repeated process of salting-out with 1 M NaCl, condensation by centrifugation at $14{,}000\times g$ for $20\mathrm{{min}}$ and subsequent solubilization in $0.5\mathrm{M}$ acetic acid. Thus, the collagen solution obtained was dialyzed in dialysis tubes (VISKING dialysis tubing, MWCO 12,000–14,000 RC, diameter $21\mathrm{mm}$ ) against sterile $0.1\ensuremath{\mathrm{M}}$ acetic acid and sterile distilled water and then lyophilized.
## 2.3. Characterization of PSC
## 2.3.1. SDS-polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE)
The SDS-PAGE test was performed according to the methods of Laemmli [23] with slight modifications; $5\%$ stacking gel and $8\%$ resolving gel was used and the electrophoretic patterns obtained were indicative of a subunit composition. The collagen samples were dissolved in $0.1\ensuremath{\mathrm{M}}$ acetic acid. The dissolved collagen samples were mixed at 1:1 and $1{:}2\left(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v}\right)$ ratios with the sample buffer $(0.5{\bf M}$ Tris–HCl buffer $\mathtt{p H}6.8$ which contained $30\%(\mathrm{v/v)}$ glycerol, $5\%(\mathrm{w/v})$ SDS, $10\%\ (\mathsf{v/v})$ $\upbeta$ -mercaptoethanol and $0.04\%$ $(\boldsymbol{\mathsf{w}}/\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}})$ bromophenol blue). Coomassie Brilliant Blue G-250 was used to visualize the gel after the electrophoresis. High molecular weight protein markers were used to estimate the molecular weight of the proteins. A gel documentation system (G:BOX Chemi HR16, Cambridge, USA) was used, using GeneSys software to analyse the SDS-PAGE gel.
## 2.3.2. Amino acid analysis
The amino acid composition of the collagen was analysed with a high performance amino acid analyser (Biochrom 30, Biochrom Limited, Cambridge, England). Lyophilized samples were dissolved in 6 M HCl solutions and hydrolysed at $110^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ .
The hydroxyproline content was measured using a GC/MS QqQ system (Agilent 7890A series GC system coupled with an Agilent 7000B QqQ MS, Agilent Technologies UK Ltd). Derivatization was performed according to the protocol developed by Villas-Boas et al. [24]. A Standard spectrum was first completed using the hydroxyproline standard to determine the mass spectrum of the hydroxyproline, which was later used to quantify the hydroxyproline.
## 2.3.3. Determination of denaturation temperature $(\mathrm{T}_{d})$
## 2.3.4. Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) analysis
Freeze dried products were individually mixed at a 1:5 ratio with vacuum dried potassium bromide (KBr) and pressed into pellets by a hydraulic press. The infrared spectra were obtained in the range between 4000 and $400\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ with the KBr disc method using an infrared spectrometer (JASCO FT/IR 4100, TOKYO, JAPAN).
## 2.3.5. $X\mathrm{.}$ -ray diffraction analysis
The X-ray diffraction pattern of the PSC was obtained using Cu K radiation from a rotating anode generator operated at
79 $45\mathrm{kV}$ and $30\mathrm{mA}$ in the range of $2\theta=4-60^{\circ}$ with Mono-single fil80 ter (XPERT, PRO, PANalytical, Netherland),with $0.02^{\circ}$ scanning step and $2^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ scanning speed.
## 2.3.6. Determination of heavy metal content
Lead, mercury, chromium, arsenic, zinc, cadmium, silver, copper and molybdenum heavy metals were tested using inductive coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (Optima 2000 DV, PerkinElmer Inc., USA) according to Official Methods of Analysis of AOAC International, 19th Edition (2012) [25].
## 2.4. PSC biocompatibility
The biocompatibility and cellular proliferation on the collagen gel were tested in vitro using the baby hamster kidney (BHK-21) fibroblasts cell line (VACSERA Institute, Giza, Egypt).
## 2.4.1. PSC gel formation
Acidic collagen solutions (ACS) of $0.5\%$ and $0.3\%\mathrm{(w/v)}$ of PSC in a dilute HCl solution with a pH of 3, were mixed with $5\times$ phosphate buffered saline (PBS), using a collagen solution/PBS ratio of 4:1 $(\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}}/\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}})$ , and the pH was adjusted to neutral $(7.4\pm0.2)\$ . The mixture was immediately transferred to non-coated 24-well plates and transferred to a $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ incubator for $60\mathrm{{min}}$ to initiate polymerization of the collagen.
The thermal stability of the gel was determined by evaluating the melting temperature $\left(T_{\mathrm{m}}\right)$ , according to the method described by Yunoki et al. [21]. In brief, the gels were prepared in glass tubes, placed in a water bath and the temperature was raised stepwise $(1^{\circ}\mathsf{C}/$ step) from room temperature $(25^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ and held for $15\mathrm{min}$ at each step.
## 2.4.2. Cell culture
The collagen gels were rinsed three times with PBS and then once with Hank’s Balanced Salt Solution (HBSS) before the test. BHK-21 cells were seeded on the 24-well collagen gel coated plate, containing HBSS with Phenol Red (BioWhittakerTM, Lonza, Belgium), supplemented with $10\%$ foetal bovine serum (FBS) (Gibco Invitrogen, Milan, Italy), with $1\%$ penicillin/streptomycin (SigmaAldrich, Inc., St. Louis, USA), at density of $3\times10^{4}$ cells per well. Some wells were untreated to be used as controls for the tests. The medium was changed every two days. At days 1, 2 and 3, the cells on the collagen gels were observed by an inverted light microscope (DMI3000 B; Leica, Germany). All experiments were performed in triplicate for control and each collagen gel concentration.
## 2.4.3. Scanning electron microscopic (SEM) evaluation
The collagen fibres of the $0.5\%$ gel and cell morphology seeded on it after $48\mathrm{h}$ were investigated by a high resolution scanning electron microscope (SEM; JSM-6380; Jeol, Tokyo, Japan). Specimens for the SE microscopic study were prepared according to the method described by Raub et al. [26], with slight modifications, as they were dried with filter paper, then mounted on SEM sample stages using carbon tape and sputter coated with Pd/Au using a sputter coater (SPI-MODULE; Structure Probe, Inc., West Chester, USA). The specimens were imaged at $25\mathrm{kV}$ and magnifications of 1500, 7500 and $10,000\times$ .
## 3. Results
## 3.1. SDS-PAGE pattern of PSC
The protein patterns of pepsin-solubilized collagen (PSC) from Nile Tilapia fish scales were analysed by an SDS-PAGE test (Fig. 1). The electrophoretic patterns of the Nile Tilapia scale collagen showed two closely related bands. One of these bands was thicker and had a stronger relative staining intensity than the other, with a molecular weights of approximately 128 and $123\mathrm{kDa}$ , respectively. In addition, a smaller band of a higher molecular weight (approximating $200\mathrm{kDa}$ was observed.
## 3.2. Amino acid composition
The amino acid composition of PSC of Nile Tilapia fish scales per 1000 is presented in Table 1 and compared to published data. The highest contents were observed in glycine, proline and hydroxyproline, in turn.
## 3.3. Denaturation temperature
Differential scanning calorimetric thermograms of PSC from the scales of Nile Tilapia showed that the $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ and $\Delta H$ of PSC was $32.09^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $17.97\mathrm{J}/\mathrm{g}$ respectively (Fig. 2).
## 3.4. FTIR analysis
Peak locations of FTIR spectra were assigned for PSC of Nile Tilapia (Fig. 3 and Table 2). Characteristic peaks frequencies were shown at 3431.71, 2931.27, 1643.05, 1546.63, $1448.28\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and at 1240.97.
## 3.5. X-ray diffraction analysis
X-ray spectrum of the PSC of Nile Tilapia fish scale showed two diffraction peaks at diffraction angles $(2\theta)$ of approximately $7.635^{\circ}$ , which had a sharp peak with a $d.$ -spacing of $1\bar{1}.569\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ and $19.776^{\circ}$ , which had a wide d-spacing of $4.485\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ (Fig. 4).
## 3.6. Heavy metal content
The heavy metal content $(\mathrm{mg/L})$ in the $0.5\%\mathrm{(w/v)}$ ACS showed that the concentrations of lead and cadmium were undetectable. While the concentrations of mercury, arsenic, chromium and nickel were 0.98, 2.57, 0.024 and $0.087\mathrm{mg/L}$ , respectively (Table 3).
## 3.7. PSC gel formation
A firm fibrillar collagen gel resulted. The thermal stability of the gel was indicated by its melting temperature $\left(T_{\mathrm{m}}\right)$ which is described as the temperature at which more than half of the volume of the gel melted; the melting temperature was found to be about $({\sim}42^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ .
## 3.8. Cell culture
Cellular proliferation on the $0.5\%$ gel proceeded faster than on the $0.3\%$ gel (Fig. 5). Both collagen concentrations proved to be biocompatible, evident from the active proliferation of BHK-21 cells, with no signs of toxicity.
The SE micrograph of the $0.5\%$ Nile Tilapia collagen gel is shown in Fig. 6. From the micrographs, it was evident that the Nile Tilapia collagen gel has slender fibrils and a well-formed and a regular fibril network structure.
The SE micrographs also showed that BHK-21 possess the typical elongated, spindle-shaped morphology with numerous cytoplasmic and dendritic extensions protruding from the cells, which attaches it to the collagen matrix (Fig. 7). Those extensions appeared in close contact with collagen fibrils forming focal
