## 4. Discussion
Extraction, characterization and biological evaluation of the scale collagen extracted from the Egyptian Nile Tilapia was performed to investigate the possibility of its use as a new potential biomaterial for medical applications.
In this study, the SDS-PAGE pattern of PSC from Nile Tilapia fish scales (Fig. 1) showed that the extracted collagen was composed of at least two different, closely related $\upalpha$ chains $\mathbf{\dot{\alpha}}_{\mathrm{{\alpha}}}\mathbf{1}$ and $\alpha2$ ) with molecular weights approximately 128 and $123\mathrm{kDa}$ , respectively. The band of $\upalpha1$ chain was thicker and had a higher intensity than that of the $\propto2$ chain, which suggested that the collagen extracted from the Nile Tilapia fish scales was type I collagen and composed of two $\propto1$ chains and one $\alpha2$ chain. The higher molecular weight $\upbeta$ chain (dimer form) of approximately $200\mathrm{kDa}$ , which represent inter and/or intra molecular crosslinked components of $\upalpha$ chains, was also observed. These results were in accordance with the previous reports of collagen extracted from scales of sardines, red seabream, Japanese seabass [27], deep-sea redfish [28], spotted golden goatfish [29] and carp fish scales [13], which have also been classified as type I collagen. Additionally, collagen from scales of lizard fish (Saurida spp.), horse mackerel (Trachurus japonicus), grey mullet (Mugil cephalus), flying fish (Cypselurus melanurus)  and yellowback seabream (Dentex tumifrons) from Japan were type I collagen [9].
The amino acid compositions of PSC from Nile Tilapia fish scales (Table 1) showed that glycine was the most abundant amino acid. This is in line with the fact that in order for the collagen polypeptide chain assembly into a triple helix to occur, a glycine residue, being the smallest amino acid, is always found in every third position of the polypeptide chains resulting in the (Gly-X-Y)n repeat structure that characterizes all collagens [4]. It is known that the X and Y positions can be occupied by any amino acid, but are most commonly occupied by proline and hydroxyproline, forming the most common triplet repeatings observed in most collagens (Gly-Pro-Hyp)n [30]. The hydroxyproline content was high, which indicated the high thermal stability of the extracted PSC. This was also reflected by the high $T_{\mathrm{d}}\left(32.09^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\right)$ of PSC from the Nile Tilapia. Hydroxyproline has an important role in collagen stability, mostly because it is essential to the formation of intramolecular hydrogen bonds [4]. Tryptophan was not detected in the collagen sample. Cystine was detected in the sample and were also detected in PSC and ASC from carp fish scales [13], unlike type I collagen from porcine dermis [19] and ordinary carp fish muscles [31].
The DSC thermogram of PSC from the scales of Nile Tilapia (Fig. 2) showed that $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ was $32.09^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , which was lower than that of type I collagen from calf skin $\mathrm{\Delta}T_{\mathrm{d}}=40.7^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}})$ [32]. This difference in thermostability (as represented by the difference in $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ ) between collagen of different species might be correlated to the difference in amino acid content (hydroxyproline and proline). The higher the content of amino acids, the higher the stability of the helices and the higher heat resistance of the collagen [33]. It is reported that the collagen of calf skin has a higher amino acid content (hydroxyproline and proline) than that of fish scales [19]. However, the thermostability of PSC from the Nile Tilapia was higher compared to the $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ of collagens from other marine fish scales, which range from 26 to $29^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ [9]. Lizard fishes from Japan and Vietnam showed similar ASC $T_{\mathrm{d}}s$ of 27.6 and $27.4^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , respectively. Horse mackerel from Japan had a $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ of $26.1^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , which was lower than that from Vietnam $(28.1^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ [9]. $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ of collagen from deep-sea redfish scales was $17.7^{\circ}\mathsf C$ [17] and carp scales was approximately $28^{\circ}C$ [18]. Interestingly, collagens from fresh water fishes have a higher thermal stability than that from marine fish, which may be due to the higher amino acid contents, or possibly with their living environment [19]. The results of the present study showed that PSC from the scales of Egyptian Nile Tilapia had a high hydroxyproline and proline content (118 and 127, respectively.), compared to that of porcine dermis, which might explain the high thermostability of the extracted collagen, although the $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ of PSC of Nile Tilapia was lower than that of porcine dermis $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\nabla}T_{\mathrm{d}}$ was about $40.7^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . These results were similar to what was found by Zhang et al. [13], who reported that the hydoxyproline content for PSC of carp fish scale was 109, while the hydroxyproline of Type I collagen of porcine dermis was reported to be 97, although $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ of PSC of carp fish scale was only about $29.0^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}.$ Also, the $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ of acid-solubilized collagen (ASC) of the same fish was reported to be $32.9^{\circ}\mathsf C$ although its hydroxyproline content was lower than that of the PSC.
The FTIR spectra of Nile Tilapia exhibited the characteristic peaks of amide I, II and III as well as amide A and B (Fig. 3). Amide A and amide I of collagen from the scales of grass carp were found at 3323 and $1654\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [34], while collagen from scales of deep-sea redfish exhibited the amide A and amide I band at 3296 and $1653\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [17]. Collagen from the scales of carp showed amide A and I bands at 3314 and $1650\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [18], whilst collagen from the skin of young Nile perch had amide A and I bands at 3434 and $1650\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [35]. Different band absorptions were possibly attributed to the different molecular structures between both collagens. The ratio of absorption intensities between $1240\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ (amide III) and $1448\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ band was approximately equal to 1.0, confirming the triple helix structure of the collagen extracted from the Nile Tilapia and is in accordance with Guzzi Plepis et al. [36]. The position of the band was found to be shifted to lower frequency $1546\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) as compared to the normal absorption range of amide II band $1550{-}1600{\mathrm{cm}}^{1}$ ), which suggests the existence of hydrogen bonding in each collagen.
Results obtained from the X-ray spectrum of the lyophilized collagen of the Nile Tilapia fish scales (Fig. 4) were in accordance with results obtained from lyophilized pepsin-solubilized carp fish scale collagen crystals [13]. The two detected diffraction peaks were in accordance with the characteristic diffraction peaks of collagen. The Bragg equation $d\left(\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\right)=\lambda/2\sin{\theta}\left(\lambda=1.54\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\right)$ , was employed to calculate the minimum values $(d)$ of the repeated spacings. The $d$ -spacing $[\hat{\mathsf{A}}]$ of the sharp peak was $\operatorname{i}1.569\mathring\mathrm{A}$ and that of the wide peak was $4.485\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , which were related to the diameter of the tri-helix collagen molecule and the single left-hand helix chain. The extracted collagen had a tri-helix structure [13].
The XRD analysis and FTIR spectra confirmed that the collagen extracted from the Nile Tilapia fish scales had a triple helix structure.
Currently, pollution of the aquatic environment is a serious and growing problem throughout the world. Industrial, agricultural and commercial chemical wastes are now discharged in increasing amounts into the aquatic environment, leading to various detrimental effects on aquatic organisms, including fish [37]. The River Nile is the life artery of Egypt and considered the main source for fishing, drinking, irrigation and navigation. It continuously receives waste water discharges from agricultural, sewage and industrial sources. According to the National Water Research Center [38], the River Nile from Aswan to Al-Kanater Barrage receives wastewater discharges from 124 point sources, of which 67 are agricultural drains and the remainder are industrial sources. Fish are often at the top of the aquatic food chain, meaning that metals accumulate in them to concentrations much time higher than that in the water and sediment [37].
The contents of heavy metal impurities in the $0.5\%\mathrm{(w/v)}$ ACS (Table 3) was calculated, to demonstrate whether they would exceed the permissible limits or not. Lead and cadmium were undetectable in the $0.5\%\mathrm{(w/v)}$ ACS. However, mercury, arsenic, chromium and nickel were detected.
The U.S. Pharmacopeial Convention (USP) specifies limits for the amounts of elemental impurities in drug products, including the amount of elemental impurities present in daily dose (permitted daily exposure based on a $50~\mathrm{Kg}$ person) and in a large volume parenteral (LVP—daily dose greater than $100\mathrm{{mL}}$ ) [39].
The International Organization for Standardization (ISO) 9917-1 [40] states that As and Pb contents in dental water-based cements should be less than 2 and $100\mathrm{ppm}$ , respectively. However, ISO 9917-1 does not regulate any other heavy metals than As and Pb. Unfortunately, there is no standard regulation to specify limits for the amounts of elemental impurities and heavy metals in biomaterials intended for biomedical applications. Therefore, there is still a need to investigate the permissible limits for elemental impurities present in biomaterials, according to its intended application, form of application (scaffold, membrane, gel, sponge, etc.) and rate of in-vivo degradation.
When soluble collagen is brought to physiological conditions of neutral pH and warmed to physiologic temperatures $(20-37^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ , collagen fibril formation in vitro occurs spontaneously by selfassembly of collagen molecules [41]. Fibrils formed in vitro are able to construct regular fibril network structures (Fig. 6). The self-assembly of collagen into fibrils varies in length and diameter depending on the source and type of collagen, extraction method and self-assembly conditions (e.g., variations in temperature, phosphate buffer, protein concentration and the extraction method) [42]. The melting temperature $\left(T_{\mathrm{m}}\right)$ of Nile Tilapia collagen fibrillar gel was estimated to be approximately $42^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , which indicated that it was possible to use at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ as a cellular matrix without the need for any physical or chemical means of cross-linking to improve the thermal stability.
The concentration of collagen monomers used to polymerize fibrillar collagen networks determines the density of the matrix. In this study; faster proliferation of BHK-21 cells seeded on the higher gel concentration $(0.5\%)$ was evident compared to the speed of the cells seeded on the lower gel concentration of $0.3\%$ (Fig. 5); both were found to be biocompatible with no signs of toxicity.
From the current study, it is clear that there is a strong tendency of cells to adhere to wells coated with collagen as opposed to untreated wells; cells tend to adhere to collagen polymers stronger than to plastic surfaces. Additionally, BHK-21 grows better on collagen coated wells than on untreated wells, and the number of cells on the treated wells was more than on untreated wells. These results are in accordance with Najafi et al. [43], though they also added different concentrations of intact or hydrolysed collagen to the media, thereby increasing collagen concentration to a certain limit, which led to cell growth stimulation. However, higher concentrations acted as an inhibitor and reduced cell growth.
Collagen and its integrin binding domains (e.g., Arg-Gly-Asp (RGD) sequences) assist in the maintenance of attachmentdependent cell types in the culture, where fibroblasts grown on collagen matrices appeared to differentiate in a way that mimics in vivo cellular activity and exhibits a nearly identical morphology and metabolism. This suggests that type I collagen can serve as tissue-regeneration scaffolds for cellular constructs [44].
BHK-21 on top of $0.5\%$ collagen gel possessed an elongated spindle-shaped morphology (Fig. 7) typical of fibroblast cells, with numerous cytoplasmic and dendritic extensions (Fig. 8). Jiang et al. [45] stated that:
“Dendritic extensions of fibroblasts began to protrude as early as cells could be observed to attach to the collagen matrix (5 min). Subsequently, the extensions increase in length and penetrate into the porous collagen network, eventually becoming difficult to distinguish by scanning electron microscopy from the collagen fibrils themselves”.
Such entanglement of the fibroblast extensions with the collagen fibrils is unique to 3D matrices and resulted in a strong cell anchorage [45]. Zhou et al. [10] investigated the effect of electrospun Tilapia skin collagen nanofibers on the adhesion and proliferation of human keratinocytes (HaCaTs) and human dermal fibroblasts (HDFs) in vitro, it was found that the cells were able to firmly adhere to the prepared nanofibers, with an increased proliferation rate when compared to the control group. Based on the results of this study, the authors suggested that the prepared electrospun collagen nanofibers could have future application in skin regeneration.
## 5. Conclusion
This study was the first to extract, purify, characterize and perform preliminary biological evaluation of type I collagen from Egyptian Nile Tilapia fish scales. In conclusion, the present study demonstrated that the following: PSC of Nile Tilapia had a $T_{\mathrm{d}}$ of $32.09^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , which is high compared to that from other fish species; Nile Tilapia collagen fibrillar gel can be used at $37^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ as a cellular matrix, without any physical or chemical means of cross-linking, given by its melting temperature $(42^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ ; and Egyptian Nile Tilapia scale collagen is biocompatible and showed no signs of toxic effects on cultured fibroblasts. Egyptian Nile Tilapia fish scales could represent a good candidate as a potential biomaterial in tissueengineering applications after more experimental investigations.
